  data:  # 3c45
    kind: work_item  # 3c45
    title: "io-boundaries: a command_launch boundary reports BINARY control-character blobs and an EMPTY STRING as primitive names (sbt) -- user-facing output, and an empty name matches anything on short-name lookup"  # 3c45
    status: todo_hard  # 3c45
    priority: 2  # 3c45
    parent:  # 3c45
    tags:  # 3c45
    - io_boundary  # 3c45
    - analysis_quality  # 3c45
    isbefore: []  # 3c45
    duplicate_of: []  # 3c45
    not_duplicate_of: []  # 3c45
    pr_ref:  # 3c45
    description: "FILED 2026-09-09, incidental to WI-kurug / WI-fasoz. Record:\n~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/t0_sizing_09092026/.\n\nsbt's io-boundaries output carries a `command_launch` boundary whose\n`primitives_used` contains BINARY CONTROL-CHARACTER BLOBS:\n\n    '\\x06\\x16\\x1e\\x066\\x06VF\\x06Q'\n    '\\x1e\\x0ez4'\n    '5\\x0e*Р3'\n    ''                      <- also an EMPTY STRING\n\nalongside legitimate shell command names (awk, base64, basename, cat, curl,\ncut, cygpath). Of sbt's 70 reported primitives, 50 are bare names and these are\namong them.\n\nWHY IT IS A DEFECT AND NOT COSMETIC:\n  * `primitives_used` is USER-FACING output. A primitive named with control\n    characters is not something a developer or an agent can act on, and an\n    EMPTY-STRING primitive name will match sloppily anywhere a consumer tests\n    membership by short name.\n  * It means something is decoding non-source bytes as a command. sbt vendors\n    binary launcher artifacts; the command-launch detector appears to be\n    reading them. Whatever the path, a binary blob should never survive to a\n    primitive name.\n  * The empty string is the more dangerous of the two: `\"\" in used` and short-\n    name matching against `\"\"` are the shapes that silently score a row as\n    reached.\n\nMEASURED, not reported second-hand: found while reading sbt's own iob.json\nduring WI-fasoz, and it is the reason that item's instrument dropped bare-name\nmatching in favour of qualified names only.\n\nSCOPE: the fix is a validity gate on command names at whatever point\n`command_launch` extracts them -- reject empty, reject non-printable. Whether\nthe upstream decode should also be prevented is the second question and needs\nthe actual file identified; sbt is the reproducer.\n\nNOT AN INV-linub INSTANCE. Filed separately because it is a data-hygiene defect\nin the boundary reporter, not a receiver-evidence failure, and folding it into\nthe drain would misreport that drain's size."  # 3c45
    fields: {}  # 3c45
- op: discuss  # a750
  at: '2026-09-28T09:05:08Z'  # a750
  by: agent  # a750
  actor: jgstern_agent  # a750
  clock: 2  # a750
  nonce: a750  # a750
  message: "RE-VERIFIED 2026-09-28 at dev 5f80a79081. PREMISE HOLDS AND THE ROW'S OPEN QUESTION ('the actual file identified') IS ANSWERED. Record: scratchpad fisoh/iob.json (shipped io-boundaries on ~/ALL_REPOS/whole_bunch_of_repos/sbt, cold cache).\n\nREPRODUCES, AND GREW: command_launch lists 52 primitives. 7 are malformed (4 at filing):\n  '', '\\x06\\x16\\x1e\\x066\\x06VF\\x06Q', '\\x1e\\x0ez4', '5\\x0e*Р3', '�\\x19_b', 'zc\\x16X', '�g\\x16'\n\nTHE SOURCE: two SELF-EXECUTING JARs. Each is an 'sh' script prefix with a ZIP archive appended ('file': \"a /usr/bin/env sh script executable (Zip archive)\"). The bash analyzer takes them by shebang (no extension) and parses the binary tail as commands:\n  launcher-package/bin/coursier   18,500 bytes, ZIP header at byte 469    -> 5 of the 7\n  sbt-app/src/sbt-test/dependency-management/exclude-dependencies2/coursier   11,811 bytes, 'exec java -jar ... \"$0\"' then ZIP at byte 55   -> 2 of the 7\nEvery bad chain is anchored at the FILE node (bash:<path>:1-1:file:file).\n\nA SECOND SYMPTOM, not in the row: the dst IDs are malformed too. The empty name mints 'bash::0-0::external_symbol', empty path AND name slots (INV-dulah's node.id grammar), and the other ids carry the control characters stripped ('bash:6VFQ:0-0:6VFQ') while the primitive keeps them, so the id and the name disagree.\n\nMECHANISM TO REUSE: js_ts.py:6452 skips a file with a NUL byte in its first 8,192 bytes; bash.py has no equivalent. Skipping the whole file is WRONG here, though: the prefix is real shell (the second file's only line is a genuine 'exec java' launch). Truncating the source at the first NUL (or the ZIP local header) keeps the prefix and drops the payload. The row's command-name validity gate (reject empty, reject non-printable) stays worth having as the second line.\nPrior art searched (self-executing, makeself, appended archive, binary payload): none. The same shape covers makeself '.run' installers and shar archives; not measured."  # a750
